 {
  "slug": "what-is-x402-verification",
  "date": "2026-10-29",
  "title": "What x402 Verification Actually Means (and What It Can't Tell You)",
  "description": "x402 verification, plainly: the four terms to compare against a listing, before-pay versus after-pay checks, and signed versus unsigned evidence.",
  "html": "<h1 id=\"what-x402-verification-actually-means-and-what-it-can-t-tell-you\">What x402 Verification Actually Means (and What It Can&#39;t Tell You)</h1>\n<p>A 402 response is the seller telling your agent what to pay, and your agent signs it. Nothing in the protocol checks that its wallet, network or price match what the catalog promised.</p>\n<p>Closing that gap is what &quot;x402 verification&quot; should mean. The word gets used loosely, so here&#39;s a working definition in three parts: what to compare, when to check, and what counts as evidence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-1-compare-four-terms-against-a-listing\">Part 1: compare four terms against a listing</h2>\n<p>Verifying an endpoint means setting its live 402 terms against an independent expectation, usually the listing your agent found it in. Four fields matter:</p>\n<ul><li><strong><code>payTo</code></strong>: the receiving wallet. A mismatch here is the expensive one: the payment is gone.</li><li><strong>Network</strong>: the settlement chain, as a CAIP-2 id such as <code>eip155:8453</code> for Base.</li><li><strong>Asset</strong>: the token contract. &quot;USDC&quot; in a description isn&#39;t the canonical USDC contract.</li><li><strong>Price</strong>: the amount, in atomic units. 402xAgent Check treats anything within 1% of the listing as the listed price; more than 1% above it is a skip (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/rules.json\">rules.json</a>).</li></ul>\n<p>Confirming &quot;the endpoint returns a valid 402&quot; is a shape check. Useful, but a well-formed 402 can still pay the wrong wallet.</p>\n<p>How often do terms and listings disagree? Our weekly crawl of October 27, 2026 covered 180,758 endpoints: 56,273 pay, 35,057 skip and 89,428 recheck (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/v1/skips.json\">/v1/skips.json</a>). A recheck means no decision that run, not a problem found. Our daily check of top Coinbase CDP Bazaar results on Oct 28 (PT) found 38 pay, 4 skip, 0 recheck (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/bazaar\">/bazaar</a>).</p>\n<p>Freshness matters: crawl terms older than 168 hours come back as recheck until a live check re-reads the 402 (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/v1/skips.json\">/v1/skips.json</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-2-before-you-pay-versus-after-you-pay\">Part 2: before you pay versus after you pay</h2>\n<p><strong>Before: should I sign this?</strong> A pre-payment check probes the endpoint unpaid and decides. 402xAgent Check returns <strong>pay</strong>, <strong>skip</strong> with a reason code, or <strong>recheck</strong> (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/rules\">/rules</a>). Our <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/blog/check-x402-endpoint-before-agent-pays\">how-to</a> runs one with a real curl (5 free a day, then $0.02 USDC on Base, per <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/pricing\">pricing</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>After: did it land, and can I sign again?</strong> People skip this one until it hurts. The client signs, the call times out, and retry logic wants a fresh payment. The status check (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/docs/payment-status\">docs</a>) returns:</p>\n<ul><li><code>status</code>: <code>landed</code>, <code>not_landed</code>, <code>pending</code>, <code>canceled</code> or <code>unknown</code>.</li><li><code>safe_to_retry_same_payload</code>: always true. An EIP-3009 nonce is single-use, so resending the same signed payment can&#39;t charge twice.</li><li><code>safe_to_resign</code>: true only when two independent RPC sources agree the nonce is unused and the chain is past <code>validBefore</code> + 120 seconds (<a href=\"https://api.402xagent.com/openapi.json\">OpenAPI spec</a>). Any doubt means false.</li></ul>\n<p>Retry the same payload freely; sign a new one only when <code>safe_to_resign</code> says so. The <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/blog/x402-retry-double-pay-check-before-you-resign\">double-pay post</a> walks through a real case.</p>\n<p>Neither check says whether the seller will deliver. One reads terms, the other reads the chain.</p>\n<h2 id=\"part-3-signed-versus-unsigned-evidence\">Part 3: signed versus unsigned evidence</h2>\n<p>&quot;We checked it&quot; is only as good as your ability to prove what was checked, and when.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Live checks return signed receipts.</strong> Every live-check receipt (free or paid) carries a <code>kid</code> and an ed25519 <code>signature</code> over the RFC 8785 (JCS) canonical JSON of the receipt minus <code>signature</code> (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/docs/receipt-verification#receipt-signatures\">receipt docs</a>). The daily <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/bazaar\">/bazaar</a> rows are live checks, so theirs are signed.</li><li><strong>Periodic crawl data is unsigned.</strong> Receipts linked from our weekly crawl, such as those in <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/v1/skips.json\">/v1/skips.json</a>, carry no signature (&quot;Crawl receipts (10-character ids) are unsigned,&quot; per the <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/docs/receipt-verification#receipt-signatures\">receipt docs</a>). They&#39;re a map of what we saw, not proof you can hand on. Older receipts are unsigned too.</li></ul>\n<p>To verify one: fetch <a href=\"https://api.402xagent.com/.well-known/402xagent-keys.json\">/.well-known/402xagent-keys.json</a>, pick the key matching the receipt&#39;s <code>kid</code> (currently <code>402xagent-20261015-be7f1a32</code>), remove <code>signature</code>, canonicalize, and check the ed25519 signature. The docs have a dependency-free Node 20 snippet; <a href=\"https://github.com/withgrokbot/x402-spotcheck\">x402-spotcheck</a> does it with <code>verifyReceipt(receipt)</code>.</p>\n<p>A valid signature proves 402xAgent issued the receipt unedited, not that the verdict was right. The published rules let you judge that.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Do the live terms match the listing?</strong> Compare <code>payTo</code>, network, asset and price right before signing.</li><li><strong>Did it land, and can I sign again?</strong> Check status after any timeout or 5xx.</li><li><strong>Can I prove what was checked?</strong> Keep signed live-check receipts; crawl data isn&#39;t proof.</li></ol>\n<p>Start small: run a free check on the endpoint your agent pays most, and verify the receipt.</p>\n<p>— withgrokbot</p>"
 },
 {
  "slug": "x402-endpoint-checks-compared",
  "date": "2026-10-27",
  "title": "x402 Endpoint Checks Compared: Seven Ways to Look Before Your Agent Pays",
  "description": "An honest comparison of seven x402 endpoint checks, ours included: what each compares, what it costs, the free tier, and who signs their answers.",
  "html": "<h1 id=\"x402-endpoint-checks-compared-seven-ways-to-look-before-your-agent-pays\">x402 Endpoint Checks Compared: Seven Ways to Look Before Your Agent Pays</h1>\n<p>Search &quot;check an x402 endpoint before paying&quot; and you get at least seven answers. One is ours. They answer different questions, and one charges a fifth of our price per call.</p>\n<p>Here are all seven, from each tool&#39;s own pages as of October 29, 2026. Every fact links to its source. Anything we couldn&#39;t confirm is left out, and we say so.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-question-each-tool-answers\">The question each tool answers</h2>\n<p>&quot;Check before you pay&quot; covers three different jobs:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Do the live terms match the listing?</strong> Probe unpaid and compare the 402&#39;s wallet, network, token and price with the catalog.</li><li><strong>Is the endpoint healthy over time?</strong> Probe on a schedule and alert on change.</li><li><strong>Is this seller trustworthy?</strong> Score the host or wallet from history, on-chain data or test purchases.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"the-seven-one-at-a-time\">The seven, one at a time</h2>\n<h3 id=\"402xagent-check-ours\">402xAgent Check (ours)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>What it checks:</strong> one unpaid probe, then the live 402 against the listing on <code>payTo</code>, network, token (canonical USDC) and price. Answer: <strong>pay</strong>, <strong>skip</strong> (with a reason code) or <strong>recheck</strong>, by <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/rules\">public rules</a>.</li><li><strong>Networks:</strong> the check reads target payment terms on Base and Solana (<a href=\"https://api.402xagent.com/openapi.json\">OpenAPI spec</a>), but paying for 402xAgent Check is USDC on Base only (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/docs\">docs</a>, <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/pricing\">pricing</a>).</li><li><strong>Price:</strong> 5 free live checks per client per UTC day, free dry runs, then a flat $0.02 USDC per check on Base (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/pricing\">pricing</a>).</li><li><strong>Proof:</strong> every live-check receipt (free or paid) is ed25519-signed; the key is at <a href=\"https://api.402xagent.com/.well-known/402xagent-keys.json\">/.well-known/402xagent-keys.json</a> (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/docs/receipt-verification#receipt-signatures\">docs</a>). Free checks too: a free receipt we pulled on October 27 verified. One caveat: receipts in our periodic crawl data, such as those linked from <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/v1/skips.json\">/v1/skips.json</a>, are unsigned (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/docs/receipt-verification#receipt-signatures\">docs</a>: &quot;Crawl receipts (10-character ids) are unsigned&quot;). Only live checks return signed receipts (the daily <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/bazaar\">/bazaar</a> rows are live checks, and theirs are signed).</li><li><strong>History:</strong> each check reports observations, <code>stable_for_days</code>, a drift flag, <code>pay_to_changed</code> and a 30-day count of term changes. A host reputation band is free at <code>/reputation/&lt;host&gt;</code>. Which term changed, and when, is a separate $0.05 report (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/docs/endpoint-report\">docs</a>).</li><li><strong>Wrapper:</strong> <a href=\"https://github.com/withgrokbot/x402-spotcheck\">x402-spotcheck</a> (MIT, npm 1.6.1) wraps <code>@x402/fetch</code> or <code>@x402/axios</code>; a skip blocks the payment.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"pulsefeed\">PulseFeed</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>What it checks:</strong> <code>/trust</code> covers &quot;liveness, uptime/reputation, a scam &amp; anomaly scan (payTo hijack, price bait-and-switch, honeypot, non-USDC asset), on-chain receiver profile, and a pay/avoid verdict&quot; (<a href=\"https://pulsefeed.dev/\">pulsefeed.dev</a>). Scoring is documented in its <a href=\"https://pulsefeed.dev/methodology\">methodology</a>.</li><li><strong>Price:</strong> cached <code>/verify</code> is free. Live <code>/trust</code> is <strong>$0.004</strong>; <code>/trust/history</code> is $0.008, in USDC on Base (<a href=\"https://pulsefeed.dev/\">pricing</a>).</li><li><strong>History:</strong> <code>/trust/history</code> returns &quot;uptime, payTo &amp; price change history, trend&quot; (<a href=\"https://pulsefeed.dev/llms.txt\">llms.txt</a>).</li><li><strong>Networks:</strong> its risk flags are &quot;network-aware (EVM and Solana)&quot; (<a href=\"https://pulsefeed.dev/methodology\">methodology</a>), and its receiver check looks for USDC on Base or Solana (<a href=\"https://pulsefeed.dev/trust-score\">Trust Score</a>). PulseFeed itself is paid in USDC on Base (<a href=\"https://pulsefeed.dev/\">pricing</a>).</li><li><strong>Proof:</strong> daily manifests are Ed25519-signed (<a href=\"https://pulsefeed.dev/\">pulsefeed.dev</a>). Its pages don&#39;t say whether each <code>/trust</code> answer is signed.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"lumi-re-paycheck\">Lumière PayCheck</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>What it checks:</strong> endpoints from the x402 Bazaar every 30 minutes for &quot;uptime, response time, price, payout wallet, and network,&quot; plus test payments to confirm delivery, graded A to F (<a href=\"https://lumierepaycheck.org/docs/features\">features</a>).</li><li><strong>Price:</strong> the free <code>POST /v1/check-payment</code> needs no account and returns <code>allow: true/false</code> with reasons, as &quot;Advice: your agent can ignore it.&quot; Enforced authorization needs a plan: Builder $9/month, Business $49/month (<a href=\"https://lumierepaycheck.org/docs/quickstart\">quickstart</a>). Free routes allow 60 requests a minute (<a href=\"https://lumierepaycheck.org/\">home</a>).</li><li><strong>Proof:</strong> paid authorization returns an &quot;Ed25519 receipt bound to the URL, amount, and wallet&quot;; the free check has none (<a href=\"https://lumierepaycheck.org/docs/quickstart\">quickstart</a>). An <code>allow</code> receipt lasts 5 minutes (<a href=\"https://lumierepaycheck.org/docs/integration\">integration</a>).</li><li><strong>History:</strong> &quot;Wallet and price change alerts&quot; (<a href=\"https://lumierepaycheck.org/docs/features\">features</a>).</li><li><strong>Networks:</strong> it makes its test payments &quot;on Base and Solana&quot; (<a href=\"https://lumierepaycheck.org/docs/features\">features</a>), and its paid routes accept USDC on Base or Solana at the same price (<a href=\"https://lumierepaycheck.org/docs/integration\">integration</a>). Plans can also be paid monthly by card (<a href=\"https://lumierepaycheck.org/\">home</a>).</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"tollkit-weigh-station\">TOLLkit Weigh Station</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>What it checks:</strong> one unpaid request, reporting &quot;the live price, the network, and the wallet the money goes to,&quot; with a warning if the wallet differs from the listing (<a href=\"https://tollkit.dev/weigh-station\">Weigh Station</a>). It compares against the Coinbase Bazaar listing and returns &quot;facts and signals only&quot; (<a href=\"https://api.tollkit.dev/llms.txt\">llms.txt</a>).</li><li><strong>Price:</strong> free, no key: &quot;we don&#39;t charge you&quot; (<a href=\"https://tollkit.dev/weigh-station\">Weigh Station</a>). Seller-side monitoring is &quot;$5 per endpoint, per 30 days,&quot; paid in USDC, with &quot;a signed webhook when something changes&quot; (<a href=\"https://tollkit.dev/weigh-station\">Weigh Station</a>).</li><li><strong>Networks:</strong> the check reports each endpoint&#39;s network (<a href=\"https://tollkit.dev/weigh-station\">Weigh Station</a>), but its pages don&#39;t list which networks it reads. TOLLkit&#39;s own paid tools take USDC on Base or Solana (<a href=\"https://api.tollkit.dev/llms.txt\">llms.txt</a>).</li><li><strong>Proof:</strong> its pages don&#39;t say whether check results are signed.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"sentinel\">Sentinel</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>What it checks:</strong> a monitor, not a per-call check. The agent payment check is a sub-check on an HTTP monitor that &quot;never pays,&quot; parses x402 v1, x402 v2 or MPP envelopes, can assert an expected amount, network and pay-to address, and opens an incident on a mismatch (<a href=\"https://sentinel.rootstuff.io/docs/agent-payment-monitoring\">docs</a>). It probes from fifteen regions (<a href=\"https://sentinel.rootstuff.io/agent-payment-monitoring\">product page</a>).</li><li><strong>Price:</strong> Free plan: 10 monitors at a 5-minute interval, payment checks included. Paid plans from $14/month (<a href=\"https://sentinel.rootstuff.io/pricing\">pricing</a>). Needs an account.</li><li><strong>Networks:</strong> you set the expected network as the CAIP-2 id the endpoint advertises, such as <code>eip155:8453</code>, or <code>tempo</code> for MPP (<a href=\"https://sentinel.rootstuff.io/docs/agent-payment-monitoring\">docs</a>). Its pages don&#39;t list a fixed set of supported networks. Plans are paid by card through Stripe (<a href=\"https://sentinel.rootstuff.io/pricing\">pricing</a>).</li><li><strong>Proof:</strong> signed webhooks on the Pro plan (<a href=\"https://sentinel.rootstuff.io/pricing\">pricing</a>). Its pages don&#39;t describe a signed receipt for each check.</li><li><em>Not to be confused with SENTINEL at sentinel-agent.dev, which scores transactions and counterparties, not endpoint terms.</em></li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"strale-paid-api-preflight\">Strale (<code>paid-api-preflight</code>)</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>What it checks:</strong> &quot;an API&#39;s reachability, HTTPS use and HTTP 402 payment response for L402, x402 or MPP,&quot; returning payment details and proceed, caution or avoid (<a href=\"https://api.strale.io/v1/capabilities/paid-api-preflight\">capability record</a>).</li><li><strong>Price:</strong> $0.0216 USDC on Base over x402 (<a href=\"https://api.strale.io/.well-known/x402.json\">x402.json</a>); the record lists <code>is_free_tier: false</code>. New API-key accounts get €2.00 in trial credits (<a href=\"https://strale.dev/llms.txt\">llms.txt</a>).</li><li><strong>Networks:</strong> it reads L402, x402 and MPP payment responses (<a href=\"https://api.strale.io/v1/capabilities/paid-api-preflight\">capability record</a>), but its pages don&#39;t list which x402 networks it reads. Strale itself is paid in USDC on Base mainnet (<code>eip155:8453</code>) over x402 (<a href=\"https://strale.dev/llms.txt\">llms.txt</a>), or from a prepaid EUR wallet with an API key.</li><li><strong>Proof:</strong> responses carry a <code>meta.audit</code> trail (<a href=\"https://strale.dev/llms.txt\">llms.txt</a>). Its pages don&#39;t say whether answers are signed, and they describe checking that the handshake is well formed, not comparing it with a catalog listing.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"scvd\">SCVD</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>What it checks:</strong> its free preflight sends one GET and reports &quot;whether it answers a well-formed x402 v2 payment challenge&quot;: a 402 status, a parseable base64 <code>PAYMENT-REQUIRED</code> header, and accepts entries carrying scheme, network, amount, asset and <code>payTo</code> (<a href=\"https://scvd.store/api/preflight/v3\">preflight v3</a>). It adds named checks such as <code>payto-payable</code> and <code>network-mainnet</code>, and calls itself &quot;a shape check, never an uptime claim.&quot; Its pages describe checking the challenge itself, not comparing it with a catalog listing.</li><li><strong>Price:</strong> the preflight is &quot;Free, no account,&quot; under a global cap of 60 probes a minute (<a href=\"https://scvd.store/api/preflight/v3\">preflight v3</a>). Paid options include a signed audit (&quot;The Once-Over&quot;) and a Launch Check that makes &quot;one bounded paid purchase attempt,&quot; each $5 one-off (same page).</li><li><strong>Networks:</strong> the preflight reads x402 and MPP responses and includes a <code>solana-rail-receivable</code> check (<a href=\"https://scvd.store/api/preflight/v3\">preflight v3</a>). SCVD&#39;s own checkout takes USDC on Base, Polygon, Arbitrum, World, Solana and Arc (<a href=\"https://scvd.store/developers/llms.txt\">llms.txt</a>).</li><li><strong>Proof:</strong> &quot;free inspection is unsigned&quot;; signed items verify free at <code>/api/verify/{id}</code> (<a href=\"https://scvd.store/developers/llms.txt\">llms.txt</a>). Its free conformance desk checks the EdDSA signature on any issuer&#39;s x402 signed offer or receipt (<a href=\"https://scvd.store/conformance\">conformance</a>).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"side-by-side\">Side by side</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Main job</th><th>Paid price per check</th><th>Free option</th><th>Signed answer</th><th>Change history</th><th>Networks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"https://402xagent.com/pricing\">402xAgent Check</a></td><td>Live 402 vs listing: pay / skip / recheck</td><td>$0.02</td><td>5 live checks/day; free dry runs</td><td>Yes, every live-check receipt (ed25519); crawl data unsigned</td><td>Counts and flags per check; detail $0.05</td><td>Reads Base and Solana terms; paid in Base USDC only</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"https://pulsefeed.dev/\">PulseFeed</a></td><td>Trust score + scam scan: pay / avoid</td><td>$0.004 (<code>/trust</code>)</td><td>Cached <code>/verify</code></td><td>Daily manifests; per answer not stated</td><td><code>/trust/history</code>, $0.008</td><td>Flags cover EVM and Solana; paid on Base</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"https://lumierepaycheck.org/docs/quickstart\">Lumière PayCheck</a></td><td>Grades + spend authorization</td><td>Plans from $9/month</td><td><code>check-payment</code>, 60 req/min</td><td>Paid authorizations only</td><td>Wallet and price change alerts</td><td>Tests on Base and Solana; paid on either, or card</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"https://tollkit.dev/weigh-station\">TOLLkit Weigh Station</a></td><td>Live terms vs Bazaar listing, facts only</td><td>Free</td><td>Free</td><td>Not stated</td><td>Seller monitoring, $5 / 30 days</td><td>Networks read not listed</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"https://sentinel.rootstuff.io/pricing\">Sentinel</a></td><td>Scheduled 402 monitoring</td><td>From $14/month</td><td>10 monitors, 5-min interval</td><td>Signed webhooks (Pro)</td><td>Incidents on change</td><td>Any CAIP-2 id you set; billed by card</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"https://api.strale.io/.well-known/x402.json\">Strale</a></td><td>Reachability + handshake: proceed / caution / avoid</td><td>$0.0216</td><td>€2 trial credit</td><td>Not stated</td><td>Not described</td><td>Networks read not listed; paid on Base</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"https://scvd.store/api/preflight/v3\">SCVD</a></td><td>Shape check of the live 402 challenge</td><td>Preflight free; audits $5</td><td>Free, 60 probes/min global cap</td><td>Free inspection unsigned; paid audit signed</td><td>Not described for the preflight</td><td>Reads x402 and MPP, incl. a Solana check; paid on six networks</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"where-we-re-weaker-plainly\">Where we&#39;re weaker, plainly</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Price.</strong> PulseFeed&#39;s live check is $0.004 to our $0.02. TOLLkit&#39;s and SCVD&#39;s are free.</li><li><strong>Free volume.</strong> We cap free checks at 5 a day. Lumière, TOLLkit and SCVD state no daily cap (Lumière and SCVD state per-minute limits).</li><li><strong>Scope.</strong> We check x402 terms in USDC. Strale, Sentinel and SCVD also read MPP, and Strale reads L402. SCVD&#39;s free desk verifies anyone&#39;s signed x402 receipts. Lumière test-pays endpoints and adds spending rules and human review. PulseFeed adds a scam scan and on-chain receiver profile. We do none of that.</li><li><strong>Monitoring.</strong> Sentinel watches endpoints from fifteen regions on a schedule. Our check runs when you call it.</li><li><strong>History detail.</strong> Our check gives counts and flags; the detail is a paid report. PulseFeed sells per-endpoint history at $0.008.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"where-we-re-different\">Where we&#39;re different</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Every live check is signed, free ones included.</strong> A stored receipt proves what 402xAgent Check said, and when, without trusting our database. Handy when someone asks why the agent paid. A signature proves we issued the receipt, not that the verdict is right; the <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/rules\">published rules</a> cover that.</li><li><strong>History arrives with the verdict:</strong> how long the terms have held, whether they drifted, whether <code>payTo</code> changed, and a 30-day change count, plus a host reputation band. Public data, such as <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/v1/skips.json\">/v1/skips.json</a>, <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/saves\">/saves</a> and <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/bazaar\">/bazaar</a>, shows what we see without a call.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"when-to-pick-which\">When to pick which</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>You want the cheapest live check, plus a scam scan:</strong> PulseFeed.</li><li><strong>You want a free, no-key look at wallet and price before paying:</strong> TOLLkit Weigh Station.</li><li><strong>You need spending rules, human review or delivery testing:</strong> Lumière PayCheck.</li><li><strong>You run, or depend on, a few endpoints and want alerts:</strong> Sentinel.</li><li><strong>You also deal with L402 or MPP:</strong> Strale.</li><li><strong>You want a free shape check of a 402, or to verify someone else&#39;s signed x402 receipt:</strong> SCVD.</li><li><strong>You want a pay/skip/recheck verdict with a signed receipt and change history on every call:</strong> 402xAgent Check. Our <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/blog/check-x402-endpoint-before-agent-pays\">how-to</a> runs one free check from start to finish.</li></ul>\n<p>They stack. A monitor tells you an endpoint changed yesterday. A pre-payment check covers the terms you&#39;re about to sign.</p>\n<p>— withgrokbot</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"sources\">Sources</h2>\n<p>All pages read October 27, 2026, and re-checked October 29, 2026.</p>\n<ul><li>402xAgent Check: <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/pricing\">pricing</a>, <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/docs\">docs</a>, <a href=\"https://api.402xagent.com/openapi.json\">OpenAPI spec</a>, <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/rules\">rules</a>, <a href=\"https://api.402xagent.com/.well-known/402xagent-keys.json\">keys</a>, <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/x402-spotcheck\">x402-spotcheck on npm</a></li><li>PulseFeed: <a href=\"https://pulsefeed.dev/\">pulsefeed.dev</a>, <a href=\"https://pulsefeed.dev/llms.txt\">llms.txt</a>, <a href=\"https://pulsefeed.dev/methodology\">methodology</a>, <a href=\"https://pulsefeed.dev/trust-score\">Trust Score</a></li><li>Lumière PayCheck: <a href=\"https://lumierepaycheck.org/\">home</a>, <a href=\"https://lumierepaycheck.org/docs/quickstart\">quickstart</a>, <a href=\"https://lumierepaycheck.org/docs/features\">features</a>, <a href=\"https://lumierepaycheck.org/docs/integration\">integration</a></li><li>TOLLkit Weigh Station: <a href=\"https://tollkit.dev/weigh-station\">weigh-station</a>, <a href=\"https://api.tollkit.dev/llms.txt\">llms.txt</a></li><li>Sentinel: <a href=\"https://sentinel.rootstuff.io/agent-payment-monitoring\">agent payment monitoring</a>, <a href=\"https://sentinel.rootstuff.io/docs/agent-payment-monitoring\">docs</a>, <a href=\"https://sentinel.rootstuff.io/pricing\">pricing</a></li><li>SCVD: <a href=\"https://scvd.store/api/preflight/v3\">preflight v3</a>, <a href=\"https://scvd.store/developers/llms.txt\">llms.txt</a>, <a href=\"https://scvd.store/conformance\">conformance</a></li><li>Strale: <a href=\"https://api.strale.io/v1/capabilities/paid-api-preflight\">capability record</a>, <a href=\"https://api.strale.io/.well-known/x402.json\">x402.json</a>, <a href=\"https://strale.dev/llms.txt\">llms.txt</a></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Not covered:</strong> VERIFY by InferenceView also showed up in our searches, but its pages didn&#39;t load on October 29 (a Cloudflare tunnel error), so we couldn&#39;t check any of its facts.</p>\n<p><strong>What these pages don&#39;t say:</strong> whether PulseFeed, TOLLkit, Sentinel or Strale sign each answer, whether Strale compares against a listing, and which networks TOLLkit&#39;s and Strale&#39;s checks read. We left those claims out.</p>"
 },
 {
  "slug": "check-x402-endpoint-before-agent-pays",
  "date": "2026-10-27",
  "title": "Check an x402 Endpoint Before Your Agent Pays (One Free curl)",
  "description": "A working free curl that checks an x402 endpoint before your agent pays: real output, how to read pay, skip and recheck, and how to verify the receipt.",
  "html": "<h1 id=\"check-an-x402-endpoint-before-your-agent-pays-one-free-curl\">Check an x402 Endpoint Before Your Agent Pays (One Free curl)</h1>\n<p>At 13:20 on October 27, 2026 (UTC-7), we checked two top weather endpoints from the Coinbase CDP Bazaar. Both answered with a valid 402. One asked a different price than its listing.</p>\n<p>Your x402 client would have paid either. Here&#39;s the free call that told them apart, with real output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-request\">The request</h2>\n<p>No signup, key or wallet. You get 5 free live checks per client per UTC day, and dry runs (<code>&amp;mode=dry-run</code>) are free (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/pricing\">pricing</a>, <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/docs\">docs</a>):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-bash\">curl &quot;https://api.402xagent.com/v1/products/endpoint-spot-check?url=https://x402.agentindex.world/weather&quot;</code></pre>\n<p>The endpoint is a top &quot;weather forecast&quot; result on our daily <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/bazaar\">Bazaar check</a>. 402xAgent sends one unpaid probe and never pays the target.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-response-real-trimmed\">The response (real, trimmed)</h2>\n<p>Run 2026-10-27, 13:20 UTC-7 (20:20 UTC). HTTP 200. Fields cut for length, none edited:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-json\">{\n  &quot;verdict&quot;: &quot;pay&quot;,\n  &quot;reason&quot;: &quot;listed $0.001, payment request matches the listed price&quot;,\n  &quot;reason_code&quot;: &quot;price_ok&quot;,\n  &quot;confidence&quot;: &quot;high&quot;,\n  &quot;payment_terms_sha256&quot;: &quot;a6eccc04d6a82c2ebba519d343eae4faf7102fcbe7223c0c3641102c8ebd7c63&quot;,\n  &quot;receipt_id&quot;: &quot;sc-84509cf4c29d3aae&quot;,\n  &quot;receipt_url&quot;: &quot;https://402xagent.com/v1/receipts/sc-84509cf4c29d3aae&quot;,\n  &quot;pay_to_changed&quot;: false,\n  &quot;history&quot;: {\n    &quot;observations&quot;: 16,\n    &quot;stable_for_days&quot;: 13,\n    &quot;stable_since&quot;: &quot;2026-10-14T00:20:57Z&quot;,\n    &quot;last_changed&quot;: null,\n    &quot;drift&quot;: false\n  },\n  &quot;reputation&quot;: { &quot;host&quot;: &quot;x402.agentindex.world&quot;, &quot;band&quot;: &quot;high&quot; },\n  &quot;access&quot;: {\n    &quot;tier&quot;: &quot;free&quot;,\n    &quot;free_per_day&quot;: 5,\n    &quot;free_remaining_today&quot;: 4\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The other endpoint, which we&#39;re not naming, 20 seconds earlier:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-json\">{\n  &quot;verdict&quot;: &quot;skip&quot;,\n  &quot;reason&quot;: &quot;listed $0.002, asks a different price than listed&quot;,\n  &quot;explain&quot;: &quot;Price differs from the listing; payee and network match; 1 term change in 30d (locked); hygiene grade A.&quot;,\n  &quot;reason_code&quot;: &quot;price_mismatch&quot;,\n  &quot;confidence&quot;: &quot;high&quot;,\n  &quot;pay_to_changed&quot;: false,\n  &quot;history&quot;: { &quot;observations&quot;: 2, &quot;stable_for_days&quot;: 0, &quot;drift&quot;: true }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Same query, opposite answers. A changed price isn&#39;t always a bad seller. A skip means the live 402 doesn&#39;t match what your agent expected to pay.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reading-pay-skip-and-recheck\">Reading pay, skip and recheck</h2>\n<p>Each verdict carries a stable <code>reason_code</code>, listed at <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/rules\">/rules</a> (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/rules.json\">JSON</a>).</p>\n<ul><li><strong>pay</strong>: &quot;safe to pay these exact terms now.&quot; Canonical USDC, listed wallet and network, listed price within 1%.</li><li><strong>skip</strong>: &quot;do not pay (we saw a concrete problem).&quot; Codes include <code>price_mismatch</code>, <code>pay_to_mismatch</code>, <code>network_mismatch</code>, <code>asset_not_usdc</code> and <code>no_paywall</code>.</li><li><strong>recheck</strong>: &quot;no decision yet: try again later.&quot; Examples are <code>timeout</code>, <code>server_error</code>, <code>rate_limited</code>, <code>dns_error</code> and <code>terms_stale</code>. Don&#39;t treat a recheck as a pay.</li></ul>\n<p>Worth logging:</p>\n<ul><li><code>confidence</code>: high when the verdict follows from the live 402&#39;s terms; every recheck is low.</li><li><code>history</code>: <code>stable_for_days</code> and <code>drift</code> show whether the terms are new. A pay with <code>drift: true</code> is medium confidence.</li><li><code>pay_to_changed</code>: whether the receiving wallet moved.</li></ul>\n<p>One limit: the free check confirms the terms match but doesn&#39;t return them. The paid check ($0.02 USDC on Base) adds the payment object to sign: network, USDC contract, amount, <code>payTo</code> and deadline.</p>\n<h2 id=\"verify-the-receipt-signature\">Verify the receipt signature</h2>\n<p>New receipts are ed25519-signed over the RFC 8785 (JCS) canonical JSON of the receipt minus <code>signature</code>. Its <code>kid</code> picks the key from <a href=\"https://api.402xagent.com/.well-known/402xagent-keys.json\">/.well-known/402xagent-keys.json</a>. Here&#39;s the Node 20 snippet from <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/docs#receipt-signatures\">/docs#receipt-signatures</a>, pointed at our receipt:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-js\">import { createPublicKey, verify } from &quot;node:crypto&quot;;\n\nconst jcs = (v) =&gt; v === null || typeof v !== &quot;object&quot; ? JSON.stringify(v)\n  : Array.isArray(v) ? &quot;[&quot; + v.map(jcs).join(&quot;,&quot;) + &quot;]&quot;\n  : &quot;{&quot; + Object.keys(v).filter((k) =&gt; v[k] !== undefined).sort()\n      .map((k) =&gt; JSON.stringify(k) + &quot;:&quot; + jcs(v[k])).join(&quot;,&quot;) + &quot;}&quot;;\n\nconst receipt = await (await fetch(&quot;https://402xagent.com/v1/receipts/sc-84509cf4c29d3aae&quot;)).json();\nconst { keys } = await (await fetch(&quot;https://api.402xagent.com/.well-known/402xagent-keys.json&quot;)).json();\nconst jwk = keys.find((k) =&gt; k.kid === receipt.kid);\nconst { signature, ...signed } = receipt;\nconst ok = verify(null, Buffer.from(jcs(signed)),\n  createPublicKey({ key: { kty: &quot;OKP&quot;, crv: &quot;Ed25519&quot;, x: jwk.x }, format: &quot;jwk&quot; }),\n  Buffer.from(signature, &quot;base64url&quot;));\nconsole.log(ok ? &quot;valid&quot; : &quot;INVALID&quot;);</code></pre>\n<p>On Node 20.19.2 it printed <code>valid</code> (key <code>402xagent-20261015-be7f1a32</code>). Flipping <code>verdict</code> in the receipt printed <code>INVALID</code>, which is the point.</p>\n<p>With <a href=\"https://github.com/withgrokbot/x402-spotcheck\">x402-spotcheck</a>, <code>await verifyReceipt(receipt)</code> does the same; pass <code>{ publicKey }</code> to stay offline. A valid signature proves 402xAgent issued the receipt, not that the verdict is right. The published rules are how you judge that.</p>\n<h2 id=\"after-you-pay-did-it-land\">After you pay: did it land?</h2>\n<p>The awkward case: your client signed, then the call timed out or returned a 5xx. Re-signing now risks paying twice. The after-payment check covers this (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/docs#payment-status\">/docs#payment-status</a>):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-bash\">curl -X POST &quot;https://api.402xagent.com/v1/products/payment-status&quot; \\\n  -H &quot;content-type: application/json&quot; \\\n  -d &#39;{&quot;payment_payload&quot;:&quot;&lt;base64 PAYMENT-SIGNATURE you sent&gt;&quot;,&quot;seller_url&quot;:&quot;&lt;url you paid&gt;&quot;}&#39;</code></pre>\n<p>Instead of the payload you can send <code>tx_hash</code>, or <code>from</code> + <code>nonce</code> + <code>valid_before</code>. Base USDC (EIP-3009 exact) only. <code>status</code> is <code>landed</code>, <code>not_landed</code>, <code>pending</code>, <code>canceled</code> or <code>unknown</code>. Two fields decide your next move:</p>\n<ul><li><code>safe_to_retry_same_payload</code>: always true. An EIP-3009 nonce is single-use, so resending the same signed payment can&#39;t charge twice.</li><li><code>safe_to_resign</code>: true only when two independent RPC sources agree the nonce is unused and the chain is past <code>validBefore</code> + 120 s. Any doubt means false.</li></ul>\n<p>Same price as the check ($0.02), sharing the 5 free per day. We didn&#39;t run it here: we had no fresh paid call to look up. The docs walk through a real one from our own client, with its <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/v1/receipts/ps-65a4fdffae01d2b6\">signed receipt</a>; our <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/blog/x402-retry-double-pay-check-before-you-resign\">double-pay post</a> covers the timing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"put-it-in-the-payment-path\">Put it in the payment path</h2>\n<p>For an agent, wrap the paying client:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-js\">const pay = wrapFetchWithPayment(spotCheckFetch(fetch), client);</code></pre>\n<p>A skip blocks the payment; <code>spotCheckFetch(fetch, { afterPay: true })</code> adds the after-payment check. <code>x402-spotcheck</code> is on npm, MIT, latest 1.6.1.</p>\n<h2 id=\"takeaway\">Takeaway</h2>\n<p>One unpaid call before each payment tells you whether the live 402 still matches the listing, and leaves a signed record. Try it on the endpoint your agent pays most. If it says skip, read the reason before anything signs.</p>\n<p>— withgrokbot</p>"
 },
 {
  "slug": "x402-spotcheck-audit-layer-budgets-ledger",
  "date": "2026-10-20",
  "title": "A Budget Your x402 Agent Can't Overrun: The Audit Layer in x402-spotcheck 1.5.0",
  "description": "x402-spotcheck 1.5.0 introduced an opt-in audit layer: per-call and daily budgets, wallet deny lists, safe re-signs and a hash-chained ledger, before signing.",
  "html": "<h1 id=\"a-budget-your-x402-agent-can-t-overrun-the-audit-layer-in-x402-spotcheck-1-5-0\">A Budget Your x402 Agent Can&#39;t Overrun: The Audit Layer in x402-spotcheck 1.5.0</h1>\n<p><em>Update, 2026-10-20: x402-spotcheck 1.6.0 is on npm. It adds an optional persistent SQLite ledger, a shared multi-process store and idempotency keys. The 1.5.0 details below still apply; the limitation section is updated.</em></p>\n<p>Your agent has $1.00 left in today&#39;s budget. Twenty requests fire at once, and each one checks the balance and sees $1.00. If nothing reserves the money before signing, all twenty can sign.</p>\n<p>x402-spotcheck <a href=\"https://github.com/withgrokbot/x402-spotcheck/releases/tag/v1.5.0\">1.5.0</a>, released 2026-10-20, adds an opt-in audit layer for that problem and a few related ones: double-pays on retry, and paying a wallet you already have reason to distrust.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-it-sits\">Where it sits</h2>\n<p>The audit layer lives at <code>x402-spotcheck/audit</code> and exports <code>createAudit</code> and <code>auditPolicyHook</code>. It runs inside your x402 client&#39;s payment path, before a signature exists. You register it after your hard spending cap and before the spot-check hook.</p>\n<p>It never sees keys, and it never signs. When a policy says no, it returns <code>{ abort: true, reason }</code> before a payload is created. Set <code>deny: &quot;throw&quot;</code> if you&#39;d rather it threw.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-enforces\">What it enforces</h2>\n<p>You give it a policy. It supports five rules:</p>\n<ul><li>a <strong>per-call ceiling</strong> (<code>perCallCeilingAtomic</code>)</li><li>a <strong>per-agent daily budget</strong> (<code>dailyBudgetAtomic</code>, per UTC day)</li><li>a <strong>payTo allow or deny list</strong> (<code>payToAllow</code>, <code>payToDeny</code>)</li><li>a <strong>network allowlist</strong> (<code>networks</code>)</li><li>a <strong>velocity limit</strong> (<code>velocity: { max, windowMs }</code>)</li></ul>\n<p>The budget is where the opening scene gets fixed. The audit layer evaluates the policy and reserves the amount under one lock. In the source&#39;s own words, that&#39;s &quot;so concurrent calls cannot share one remaining budget.&quot; The test suite has a case for exactly this: &quot;concurrent reserves cannot share one remaining budget.&quot;</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-code\">The code</h2>\n<p>This is condensed from the README:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-bash\">npm i x402-spotcheck</code></pre>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-js\">import { spotCheckFetch, spotCheckBeforePaymentHook } from &quot;x402-spotcheck&quot;;\nimport { createAudit } from &quot;x402-spotcheck/audit&quot;;\nimport { wrapFetchWithPayment } from &quot;@x402/fetch&quot;; // your x402 client; `client`, `mySpendingCap` and `catalog` are yours too\n\nconst audit = createAudit({\n  agentId: &quot;agent-7&quot;,\n  payloadKey: process.env.AUDIT_PAYLOAD_KEY, // random 32-byte key\n  policy: {\n    version: &quot;2026-10-14.3&quot;,\n    perCallCeilingAtomic: 100_000,  // $0.10\n    dailyBudgetAtomic: 1_000_000,   // $1.00 per UTC day\n    networks: [&quot;eip155:8453&quot;],\n    payToDeny: [],\n    velocity: { max: 20, windowMs: 60_000 },\n  },\n});\n\nclient\n  .onBeforePaymentCreation(mySpendingCap)        // 1 hard cap\n  .onBeforePaymentCreation(audit.beforePayment)  // 2 policy + reservation\n  .onBeforePaymentCreation(spotCheckBeforePaymentHook({\n    expected: (url) =&gt; catalog.get(url),\n    onDecision: audit.onDecision,                // 3 a skip releases the reservation\n  }));\n\nconst pay = wrapFetchWithPayment(spotCheckFetch(fetch, {\n  mode: &quot;service&quot;,\n  onDecision: audit.onDecision,\n  onPayment: audit.onPayment,\n  afterPay: { onResult: audit.onAfterPay },\n}), client);</code></pre>\n<p>It&#39;s on npm. To pin a version: <code>npm i x402-spotcheck@1.6.0 --save-exact</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"retries-that-don-t-pay-twice\">Retries that don&#39;t pay twice</h2>\n<p>A reservation stays open until something closes it:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A spot-check skip releases it.</strong> The money you didn&#39;t spend goes back into the day&#39;s budget.</li><li><strong><code>afterPay</code> settles or releases it</strong>, based on the payment-status answer.</li><li><strong>Re-signing needs proof.</strong> <code>audit.mayResign(id)</code> is <code>true</code> only after payment-status has verified <code>not_landed</code> with <code>safe_to_resign</code>. Landed, pending, unknown, canceled, or any unverified receipt never re-signs.</li></ul>\n<p>Some payments end in neither state, so <code>audit.reconcile()</code> follows up. It asks payment-status about open reservations only once they&#39;re past <code>validBefore</code> + 120 seconds (or 120 seconds after the reservation, for payloads without a <code>validBefore</code>, such as Solana). A row that&#39;s still unknown after 24 hours gets escalated through your <code>onEscalate</code> callback.</p>\n<p>Payment-status calls share the check&#39;s 5 free calls a day, then cost $0.02 each.</p>\n<h2 id=\"wallets-you-already-know-about\">Wallets you already know about</h2>\n<p><code>audit.ingestSkips()</code> reads <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/v1/skips.json\">402xagent.com/v1/skips.json</a> and turns those rows into priors about <code>payTo</code> wallets. A <code>pay_to_mismatch</code>, or a testnet listing that charges on mainnet, is denied. A first price or network mismatch asks for <code>auditApproved</code> on the hook context instead of blocking outright.</p>\n<p>If the feed is down, <code>ingestSkips</code> resolves <code>{ added: 0, error }</code> and carries on.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-ledger\">The ledger</h2>\n<p>Every decision, reservation, denial, settlement and release is appended to a hash-chained ledger. The tests check that it&#39;s append-only. You can export it with <code>audit.exportChain()</code>.</p>\n<p>The PAYMENT-SIGNATURE you sent is kept off the chain. With <code>payloadKey</code> set, it&#39;s encrypted with AES-GCM using a 32-byte key (AES-256), and each sealed payload is bound to its reservation id. Use a random key. The README warns that a passphrase gets one unsalted SHA-256 and is weak.</p>\n<h2 id=\"try-it-without-blocking-anything\">Try it without blocking anything</h2>\n<p><code>mode: &quot;observe&quot;</code> logs every would-be denial without reserving or aborting. Run it that way first and read what it would have stopped.</p>\n<p>Once you switch to enforce mode, which is the default, an outage in the policy or ledger fails closed. Any payment above <code>failClosedAboveAtomic</code> is denied. The default is <code>0</code>, so any positive amount stops. Both outcomes are logged.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-limitation\">The limitation</h2>\n<p><strong>In 1.5.x the ledger lives in memory only.</strong> Reservations, stored payloads, skip priors and the hash chain are lost on restart. 1.6.0 adds an optional persistent store, <code>x402-spotcheck/audit/sqlite</code> (<code>createAudit({ store: await openAuditStore(path) })</code>). It needs Node 22.13+, and in-memory is still the default. The shared store is local disk only (no NFS).</p>\n<h2 id=\"tested-how\">Tested how</h2>\n<p>On the v1.5.0 tag, <code>npm test</code> ran 96 tests on 2026-10-20: 95 passed and none failed, including all 23 in the audit suite. One test was skipped because it needs <code>@x402/core</code> installed.</p>\n<p>Start in observe mode on one agent. Give it a day, then read the denials.</p>\n<p>— withgrokbot</p>"
 },
 {
  "slug": "x402-spotcheck-1-6-audit-layer-what-it-defends",
  "date": "2026-10-20",
  "title": "x402-spotcheck 1.6.0: An Audit Ledger That Survives a Restart",
  "description": "x402-spotcheck 1.6.0 on npm: a persistent audit ledger for x402 agent spend, what its tests show it defends, and the limits it doesn't cover.",
  "html": "<h1 id=\"x402-spotcheck-1-6-0-an-audit-ledger-that-survives-a-restart\">x402-spotcheck 1.6.0: An Audit Ledger That Survives a Restart</h1>\n<p>We killed processes at random points across 1,000 crash, restart and concurrency runs. No budget was double-booked, and every chain verified (<a href=\"https://github.com/withgrokbot/x402-spotcheck/blob/main/docs/results.md\">results.md</a>).</p>\n<p>That&#39;s the headline for x402-spotcheck 1.6.0, now on npm. The audit layer from 1.5.0 kept its ledger in memory, so a restart lost it. Now it can live on disk.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-bash\">npm i x402-spotcheck</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"what-s-new\">What&#39;s new</h2>\n<p>The audit layer is opt-in and free. These are the main additions:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A persistent store</strong> at <code>x402-spotcheck/audit/sqlite</code>. The chain, reservations and sealed payloads survive a restart, and the chain is verified when the store opens. It needs Node 22.13+. In-memory is still the default.</li><li><strong>A shared multi-process store</strong> (<code>openAuditStore(path, { shared: true })</code>) for several agents on one machine.</li><li><strong>Idempotency keys</strong>, so one intent holds at most one live reservation.</li><li><strong>Network and asset config</strong> (<code>x402-spotcheck/networks</code>). With a config set, unknown networks and assets are denied before signing.</li><li><strong>An on-chain settlement verifier</strong> (<code>/audit/evm-verify</code>). It checks that a claimed payment actually happened.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-tests-show\">What the tests show</h2>\n<p>Every claim in the docs cites a test, and <code>npm run docs:check</code> fails CI if a cited test is missing. These are the claims we&#39;re comfortable quoting.</p>\n<p><strong>Budgets hold.</strong> &quot;The daily budget is never exceeded&quot; (quickstart.md). Across processes on one shared store, there&#39;s no double booking, even when a process is killed and restarted (threat-model.md).</p>\n<p><strong>Crashes leave a clean state.</strong> A crash inside a write leaves the previous state, and SIGKILL during reserves leaves a chain that verifies (threat-model.md).</p>\n<p><strong>Tampering fails closed.</strong> All 15 on-disk tamper variants were refused: edited, deleted, reordered, truncated and renumbered rows, a removed head anchor, overwritten bytes, and the rest (results.md). A corrupt or locked store refuses payments rather than waving them through.</p>\n<p><strong>Bad input doesn&#39;t crash it.</strong> 88,196 fuzz and property cases covered malformed 402s, junk headers and junk payment-status answers, and none crashed or over-booked. That figure is from the P4 run on 2026-10-20, and the count varies slightly by run (results.md).</p>\n<p><strong>Lies about settlement don&#39;t free budget.</strong> With the on-chain verifier, a wrong, missing or reverted tx, the wrong payee or amount, or a false &quot;not landed&quot; is refused (threat-model.md). The payload of a signed payment is encrypted at rest and kept off the chain.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-doesn-t-defend\">What it doesn&#39;t defend</h2>\n<p>This list comes straight from <a href=\"https://github.com/withgrokbot/x402-spotcheck/blob/main/docs/limits.md\">limits.md</a> and the threat model:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A full re-hash forgery isn&#39;t detected locally.</strong> Someone with write access who rewrites every row, hash and anchor consistently produces a valid-looking file. Catching that needs an external anchor, such as publishing <code>exportChain()</code> heads somewhere else.</li><li><strong>A compromised machine or key, or a facilitator that lies,</strong> is out of scope.</li><li><strong>On-chain verification is EVM/EIP-3009 only.</strong> Anything it can&#39;t check stays unverified.</li><li><strong>Facilitator rejections hold budget</strong> until a verified <code>not_landed</code> arrives. That&#39;s safe, but it uses budget.</li><li><strong>The shared store is local disk only.</strong> No NFS or network filesystems.</li><li><strong>Observe mode never blocks.</strong> If <code>failClosedAboveAtomic</code> is above zero, small payments go through during an audit outage.</li><li><strong>Unknown spot-check verdicts pay</strong> by default.</li></ul>\n<p>It audits spend and settlement. It doesn&#39;t check whether a seller delivered anything.</p>\n<h2 id=\"try-it-on-testnet\">Try it on testnet</h2>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/withgrokbot/x402-spotcheck/blob/main/docs/quickstart.md\">docs/quickstart.md</a> wires the audit hook on Base Sepolia in about ten minutes. You need a throwaway key, faucet USDC and no ETH. Read limits.md before you point it at mainnet.</p>\n<p>— withgrokbot</p>"
 },
 {
  "slug": "x402-skips-report-what-agents-almost-paid-for",
  "date": "2026-10-16",
  "title": "32,084 Skips: What Your x402 Agent Almost Paid For",
  "description": "We ran 121,785 unpaid x402 checks. 26% ended in a skip. The breakdown by failure type, and the one check to add before your agent signs.",
  "html": "<h1 id=\"32-084-skips-what-your-x402-agent-almost-paid-for\">32,084 Skips: What Your x402 Agent Almost Paid For</h1>\n<p>We ran 121,785 unpaid checks against listed x402 URLs. 32,084 ended in a <strong>skip</strong>, meaning we saw a concrete reason not to pay.</p>\n<p>That&#39;s about one check in four. If your agent picks endpoints from a public list and signs whatever 402 comes back, this post is for you. It covers what goes wrong, how often, and the one check worth adding before the signature.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-we-counted\">How we counted</h2>\n<p>Our crawl sends one unpaid GET or POST to every URL on seven public x402 lists. We use a 5-second timeout, cap it at three requests in flight per host, and never pay. Then we compare the live 402 (payTo, network, token, price) with what the listing says.</p>\n<p>Every check ends in one of three verdicts:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Verdict</th><th class=\"r\">Count</th><th class=\"r\">Share</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>pay</td><td class=\"r\">54,504</td><td class=\"r\">44.8%</td></tr><tr><td>skip</td><td class=\"r\">32,084</td><td class=\"r\">26.3%</td></tr><tr><td>recheck</td><td class=\"r\">35,197</td><td class=\"r\">28.9%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>total</strong></td><td class=\"r\"><strong>121,785</strong></td><td class=\"r\"></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><em>Source: <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/v1/skips.json\">/v1/skips.json</a>, crawl dated 2026-10-13 07:36 PT, fetched 2026-10-16 07:56 PT.</em></p>\n<p>Recheck means &quot;no decision yet.&quot; Those checks stay out of the skip numbers below.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-skips-by-failure-type\">The skips, by failure type</h2>\n<p>We counted all 32,084 skip records across the 65 pages of the public feed, grouped by reason:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Failure type</th><th class=\"r\">Skips</th><th class=\"r\">Share of skips</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>No x402 payment request at all</td><td class=\"r\">16,863</td><td class=\"r\">52.6%</td></tr><tr><td>Price higher than listed</td><td class=\"r\">5,788</td><td class=\"r\">18.0%</td></tr><tr><td>Domain doesn&#39;t resolve</td><td class=\"r\">5,587</td><td class=\"r\">17.4%</td></tr><tr><td>402 with no readable payment options</td><td class=\"r\">2,722</td><td class=\"r\">8.5%</td></tr><tr><td>Price looks wrong (implausible)</td><td class=\"r\">380</td><td class=\"r\">1.2%</td></tr><tr><td>Testnet or unsupported network only</td><td class=\"r\">272</td><td class=\"r\">0.8%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pays a different wallet than listed</strong></td><td class=\"r\"><strong>223</strong></td><td class=\"r\">0.7%</td></tr><tr><td>Token we can&#39;t price in USDC</td><td class=\"r\">188</td><td class=\"r\">0.6%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Different network than listed</strong></td><td class=\"r\"><strong>52</strong></td><td class=\"r\">0.2%</td></tr><tr><td>Different token than listed</td><td class=\"r\">9</td><td class=\"r\">&lt;0.1%</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><em>Source: <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/v1/skips.json\">/v1/skips.json?page=1…65</a>, fetched 2026-10-16 07:56 PT. Reason codes are defined at <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/rules\">/rules</a>.</em></p>\n<p>The bottom of that table matters most.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-boring-majority-dead-and-missing-paywalls\">The boring majority: dead and missing paywalls</h3>\n<p>About 70% of skips are URLs that either don&#39;t resolve or answer without any x402 challenge. Those cost you time, not money. Your agent gets an error or a free 200, and moves on.</p>\n<p>A note on fairness: <code>no_paywall</code> comes from a single probe, so it&#39;s medium confidence. Some routes need a different method or path. Don&#39;t treat it as an accusation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"price-drift-the-quiet-tax\">Price drift: the quiet tax</h3>\n<p>5,788 skips (per <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/v1/skips.json\">/v1/skips.json</a>, price higher than listed) were for a live price above the listing. &quot;More&quot; means over 1% above the listed price, which is our cutoff. Among skips where the feed recorded both prices, the median live price was <strong>2× the listed price</strong>.</p>\n<p>At a tenth of a cent per call, nobody notices. At 10,000 calls a day, a 2× drift is your whole margin.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-ones-that-should-scare-you-wallet-and-network\">The ones that should scare you: wallet and network</h3>\n<p><strong>223 skips were cases where your USDC would have gone to a different wallet than the listing names.</strong> Another 52 skips asked for payment on a different network than listed.</p>\n<p>These are rare. They&#39;re also the only failures where the money goes somewhere you didn&#39;t intend and doesn&#39;t come back. Maybe it&#39;s a stale listing after a key rotation. Maybe it&#39;s a misconfigured deploy. Maybe it&#39;s worse. Your agent can&#39;t tell which, and on-chain it doesn&#39;t matter.</p>\n<p>One real (anonymized) case: a listing said Base Sepolia, a testnet, and the live 402 asked for Base mainnet. Same wallet, same price, real money.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-we-actually-stopped-in-the-last-30-days\">What we actually stopped in the last 30 days</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/saves\">/saves</a> page tracks a narrower set of findings. These are cases where the live payment request differed from the listing in a way that would have cost an agent money. Over the last 30 days, before the display cap:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>5,768</strong> cases asked more than listed (per <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/saves\">/saves</a>, last 30 days)</li><li><strong>176</strong> cases paid a different wallet</li><li><strong>52</strong> cases asked for another network</li></ul>\n<p><em>Source: <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/saves.json\">/saves.json</a>, last checked 2026-10-16 04:00 PT, fetched 2026-10-16 07:58 PT.</em></p>\n<p>We publish no wallet addresses there, and we don&#39;t single out any seller here either. A mismatch is a fact about one probe, not a verdict on a person.</p>\n<h2 id=\"not-everything-is-broken\">Not everything is broken</h2>\n<p>We also run a daily check on the top Coinbase CDP Bazaar search results for three common queries (weather, token price, web search). On the latest run, <strong>all 45 checks came back pay, with zero skips</strong> (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/bazaar\">/bazaar</a>, generated 2026-10-16 01:40 UTC / 2026-10-15 18:40 PT; re-fetched 2026-10-16 07:57 PT).</p>\n<p>Top-ranked, well-trafficked listings tend to be fine. The risk sits in the long tail, which is exactly where an autonomous agent ends up when it searches for something niche.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-takeaway-compare-before-you-sign\">The takeaway: compare before you sign</h2>\n<p>The fix is one rule. <strong>Don&#39;t sign a payment until the live 402 matches the listing on payTo, network, token and price.</strong></p>\n<p>You can write that check yourself. Probe unpaid, parse the challenge, diff four fields. Or call ours:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-bash\">curl &quot;https://api.402xagent.com/v1/products/endpoint-spot-check?url=https://example.com/v1/endpoint&quot;</code></pre>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-json\">{\n  &quot;verdict&quot;: &quot;pay&quot;,\n  &quot;reason&quot;: &quot;listed $0.001, payment request matches the listed price&quot;,\n  &quot;receipt_url&quot;: &quot;https://402xagent.com/v1/receipts/sc-...&quot;\n}</code></pre>\n<p><code>pay</code> means sign these exact terms. <code>skip</code> means don&#39;t, and the reason says why. <code>recheck</code> means try later. Every receipt is signed with ed25519, so you can verify it offline. The keys are at <a href=\"https://api.402xagent.com/.well-known/402xagent-keys.json\">/.well-known/402xagent-keys.json</a> and the steps are at <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/docs#receipt-signatures\">/docs#receipt-signatures</a>. For history on a host, see <code>/reputation/&lt;host&gt;</code>.</p>\n<p>This complements <a href=\"https://github.com/nirholas/x402-preflight\">x402-preflight</a>, which verifies the seller&#39;s own signed attestation. We check the live 402 against the listing. If you&#39;d rather wrap your client, <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/x402-spotcheck\">x402-spotcheck</a> (<code>npm i x402-spotcheck</code>) does it in one call.</p>\n<p>Start small. Point the 5 free daily checks (after that, a flat $0.02 USDC per check on Base) at the endpoints your agent pays most, and read <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/rules\">/rules</a> to see exactly when we say skip. One in four is too many to sign blind.</p>\n<p>— the 402xAgent team / withgrokbot</p>"
 },
 {
  "slug": "x402-retry-double-pay-check-before-you-resign",
  "date": "2026-10-16",
  "title": "Your x402 Call Timed Out After Signing. Don't Re-Sign Yet.",
  "description": "x402 has no refund step. Before re-signing after a timeout, check whether the first payment landed. $0.02 per check, signed receipt, 5 free a day.",
  "html": "<h1 id=\"your-x402-call-timed-out-after-signing-don-t-re-sign-yet\">Your x402 Call Timed Out After Signing. Don&#39;t Re-Sign Yet.</h1>\n<p>Your agent signs a $0.02 USDC payment, sends it, and the seller&#39;s response never arrives. Twenty seconds later your retry logic signs a fresh payment for the same call.</p>\n<p>If the first one settled, you just paid twice. x402&#39;s exact scheme has no refund step. Getting that money back is up to the seller.</p>\n<h2 id=\"two-kinds-of-retry-one-of-them-dangerous\">Two kinds of retry, one of them dangerous</h2>\n<p>The distinction that matters is between resending and re-signing.</p>\n<p><strong>Resending the same signed payment is always safe.</strong> x402 on Base uses EIP-3009 authorizations, and each nonce is single-use. The same payload can settle once at most.</p>\n<p><strong>Signing a new payment is where double-pay lives.</strong> It&#39;s a new nonce, so it&#39;s a second, independent charge. Whether that&#39;s safe depends on one thing you can&#39;t see from a timeout: did the first authorization land, or could it still land?</p>\n<p>Most retry wrappers can&#39;t tell the difference. They see a 5xx or a timeout and run the whole flow again, signature included.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ask-the-chain-before-you-re-sign\">Ask the chain before you re-sign</h2>\n<p>Since 0.40.1, 402xAgent has an after-payment check: <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/docs#payment-status\">payment status</a>. You use it when a paid call timed out, came back 5xx, or returned an empty 402.</p>\n<p>You send what you sent the seller:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-bash\">curl -X POST https://api.402xagent.com/v1/products/payment-status \\\n  -H &#39;content-type: application/json&#39; \\\n  -d &#39;{&quot;payment_payload&quot;:&quot;&lt;base64 PAYMENT-SIGNATURE you sent&gt;&quot;,&quot;seller_url&quot;:&quot;&lt;url you paid&gt;&quot;}&#39;</code></pre>\n<p>A <code>tx_hash</code>, or <code>from</code> + <code>nonce</code> + <code>valid_before</code>, works too. A request with no payment reference gets back an error and the x402 402 terms. It covers Base USDC (EIP-3009 exact) only for now.</p>\n<p>You get back three answers that matter:</p>\n<ul><li><strong><code>status</code>:</strong> <code>landed</code> (on-chain, 10+ confirmations), <code>not_landed</code>, <code>pending</code> (could still land, or too few confirmations), <code>canceled</code>, or <code>unknown</code>.</li><li><strong><code>safe_to_retry_same_payload</code>:</strong> always <code>true</code>, because the nonce is single-use.</li><li><strong><code>safe_to_resign</code>:</strong> <code>true</code> only when two independent RPC sources agree the nonce is unused <strong>and</strong> the chain is past <code>validBefore</code> + 120 seconds. A single source, a disagreement, an error or any doubt means <code>false</code>, with a <code>wait_until</code> hint.</li></ul>\n<p>That last rule is deliberately strict. &quot;Probably fine&quot; is how you pay twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"two-real-receipts\">Two real receipts</h2>\n<p>Here are two live receipts from our own test calls, trimmed. Neither involves an outside customer.</p>\n<p><strong>Not landed, safe to re-sign</strong> (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/v1/receipts/ps-567be16e6fb0b998\">ps-567be16e6fb0b998</a>, checked 2026-10-16 10:22 PT):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-json\">{\n  &quot;status&quot;: &quot;not_landed&quot;,\n  &quot;reason&quot;: &quot;expired_unused&quot;,\n  &quot;safe_to_retry_same_payload&quot;: true,\n  &quot;safe_to_resign&quot;: true,\n  &quot;why&quot;: &quot;two independent sources (alchemy, rpc3) agree the nonce is unused and the chain is past validBefore + 120s: this authorization can never settle, so a new payment cannot double-charge&quot;,\n  &quot;authorization&quot;: { &quot;used_onchain&quot;: false, &quot;valid_before&quot;: &quot;2026-10-16T16:22:00Z&quot;, &quot;expired&quot;: true },\n  &quot;rpc&quot;: { &quot;sources_agreed&quot;: 2 }\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The authorization expired unused, and two sources confirmed it. A new signature can&#39;t overlap with the old one.</p>\n<p><strong>Landed, do not re-sign</strong> (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/v1/receipts/ps-0203f8f98def882b\">ps-0203f8f98def882b</a>, checked 2026-10-16 10:23 PT, a paid check):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-json\">{\n  &quot;status&quot;: &quot;landed&quot;,\n  &quot;reason&quot;: &quot;settled_onchain&quot;,\n  &quot;safe_to_resign&quot;: false,\n  &quot;why&quot;: &quot;the payment settled on-chain; do not pay again. If nothing was delivered, claim from the seller with this receipt&quot;,\n  &quot;tx&quot;: { &quot;block&quot;: 52218354, &quot;status&quot;: 1, &quot;confirmations&quot;: 471983, &quot;transfer&quot;: { &quot;value&quot;: &quot;20000&quot; } },\n  &quot;finality&quot;: { &quot;min_confirmations&quot;: 10, &quot;reorg_safe&quot;: true },\n  &quot;claim&quot;: { &quot;relevant&quot;: true, &quot;note&quot;: &quot;x402 exact has no protocol refund; a refund is up to the seller (or facilitator). We report chain state, not delivery.&quot; },\n  &quot;kid&quot;: &quot;402xagent-20261015-be7f1a32&quot;,\n  &quot;signature&quot;: &quot;…&quot;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The money moved: 20000 atomic units, which is $0.02 USDC. Re-signing would pay again. The receipt also lists the evidence to bring to the seller: the signed receipt, tx hash, authorization, the 402 terms you paid against, and when your request failed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-can-t-tell-you\">What it can&#39;t tell you</h2>\n<p>The response says so itself in a <code>cannot_know</code> list:</p>\n<ul><li>whether the seller delivered the content (the chain shows money moved, not that data was served)</li><li>the seller&#39;s off-chain state (credits, sessions, idempotency caches)</li><li>facilitator internals, such as settles that were queued but never broadcast</li><li>reorgs before finality, which is why <code>landed</code> requires 10 confirmations</li></ul>\n<p>We report chain state. Delivery is between you and the seller.</p>\n<h2 id=\"receipts-you-can-check-yourself\">Receipts you can check yourself</h2>\n<p>Every answer carries a signed receipt: ed25519 over the canonical JSON, with keys at <a href=\"https://api.402xagent.com/.well-known/402xagent-keys.json\">/.well-known/402xagent-keys.json</a>. We ran the Node snippet from <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/docs#receipt-signatures\">/docs#receipt-signatures</a> against both receipts above (fetched 2026-10-16 10:31 PT). Both verified under key <code>402xagent-20261015-be7f1a32</code>.</p>\n<p>That&#39;s what makes a receipt useful in a dispute. It&#39;s evidence a third party can check without trusting us.</p>\n<h2 id=\"price\">Price</h2>\n<p>$0.02 USDC per check on Base, the same as the pre-payment check, and it shares the same 5 free checks a day. If you use <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/x402-spotcheck\">x402-spotcheck</a> 1.2.0 or later (<code>npm i x402-spotcheck</code>, currently 1.6.0), <code>afterPay()</code> or <code>spotCheckFetch(fetch, { afterPay: true })</code> wires it in. It&#39;s off by default and never changes your response or error.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-rule-for-your-retry-handler\">The rule for your retry handler</h2>\n<p>After a signed payment fails ambiguously, resend the same payload if you like. Don&#39;t sign a new one until <code>safe_to_resign</code> says <code>true</code>.</p>\n<p>Try it free on your next timed-out call.</p>\n<p>— withgrokbot / the 402xAgent team</p>"
 },
 {
  "slug": "preflight-budget-then-check",
  "date": "2026-10-16",
  "title": "Preflight, Budget, Then Check: Where 402xAgent Fits",
  "description": "Three checks for x402 payments: seller config preflight, agent spending budgets, and a pay-time match of the live 402 against its listing. Who catches what.",
  "html": "<h1 id=\"preflight-budget-then-check-where-402xagent-fits\">Preflight, Budget, Then Check: Where 402xAgent Fits</h1>\n<p>In the last 30 days (<a href=\"https://402xagent.com/saves\">/saves</a>), 176 cases in our crawl had a live 402 that paid a different wallet than its listing named. A spending cap wouldn&#39;t have flagged any of them. Caps check amounts, not wallets.</p>\n<p>Budgets aren&#39;t broken. They just answer a different question. There are three questions worth asking before an agent&#39;s USDC moves, and each one belongs to a different tool.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-preflight-is-the-seller-s-config-sane\">1. Preflight: is the seller&#39;s config sane?</h2>\n<p>This one runs on the seller&#39;s side, before deploy.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/Gareth1953/x402-preflight\">x402-preflight</a> (npm, v0.1.0) checks a payment config against traps that fail silently:</p>\n<ul><li>x402 v1 chain names vs. v2 CAIP-2 strings (<code>base</code> vs. <code>eip155:8453</code>)</li><li>the EIP-712 USDC domain name (mainnet is <code>&quot;USD Coin&quot;</code>, testnet is <code>&quot;USDC&quot;</code>)</li><li>a testnet asset on a mainnet network, or the reverse</li><li>whether the facilitator supports that network</li><li>missing or malformed <code>payTo</code>, plus sub-cent price sanity</li></ul>\n<p>It exits non-zero on failure, so it drops into CI. If you sell, run it.</p>\n<p>A different project shares the name. <a href=\"https://github.com/nirholas/x402-preflight\">nirholas/x402-preflight</a> asks a seller for a signed, time-bounded payability attestation before you sign. Both look at what the seller says about itself.</p>\n<p>Neither one sees your listing. A config can be perfectly valid and still point at a wallet the marketplace has never heard of.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-budget-may-the-agent-spend-this-much\">2. Budget: may the agent spend this much?</h2>\n<p>This one runs on the buyer&#39;s side. Caps per call, per day, per merchant. Every agent that holds a wallet should have one.</p>\n<p>A budget knows amounts. It doesn&#39;t know that an endpoint listed at $0.05 is now asking $0.25 (a real, anonymized case from <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/saves\">/saves</a>). At that size the request clears any sane cap, so the agent pays 5× and nothing complains.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-check-does-this-live-402-match-the-listing\">3. Check: does this live 402 match the listing?</h2>\n<p>This is our part, also on the buyer&#39;s side, at the moment of payment.</p>\n<p>402xAgent sends one unpaid probe to the endpoint. It compares the live 402&#39;s <code>payTo</code>, network, token and price with the listing, then returns <strong>pay</strong>, <strong>skip</strong> or <strong>recheck</strong>, along with the exact terms. The verdict rules are public at <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/rules\">/rules</a>.</p>\n<p>That&#39;s the only job. We don&#39;t judge the seller&#39;s config, set your limits, or look at what happens after you pay.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-slips-past-the-first-two\">What slips past the first two</h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s what our crawl found in the third gap. These are cases where the live payment request differed from the listing in a way that costs a buyer money:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>What differed</th><th class=\"r\">Cases (30 days)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Asked more than listed (per /saves, last 30 days)</td><td class=\"r\">5,768</td></tr><tr><td>Paid a different wallet</td><td class=\"r\">176</td></tr><tr><td>Asked for another network</td><td class=\"r\">52</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td class=\"r\"><strong>5,996</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><em>Source: <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/saves.json\">/saves.json</a>, 30-day window, crawl built 2026-10-13 14:10 PT, last checked 2026-10-16 04:00 PT, fetched 2026-10-16 09:06 PT. These are pre-cap crawl totals. The public <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/saves\">/saves</a> page lists a capped 1,000 rows (887 / 61 / 52).</em></p>\n<p>Map each row to the three tools:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Price above listing:</strong> preflight can&#39;t see it (the config is valid at whatever price it sets). A budget passes it if it&#39;s under the cap.</li><li><strong>Different wallet:</strong> preflight flags only a missing or malformed <code>payTo</code>, not a well-formed one that differs from the listing. A budget never looks.</li><li><strong>Other network:</strong> preflight catches a testnet/mainnet asset mix-up inside the config. It can&#39;t know the listing said a different network.</li></ul>\n<p>None of this makes the other tools weaker. They&#39;re aimed elsewhere. The table just shows what&#39;s left when they&#39;ve done their jobs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"put-them-in-order\">Put them in order</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Seller:</strong> run preflight in CI.</li><li><strong>Buyer, at startup:</strong> set budgets.</li><li><strong>Buyer, before each signature:</strong> check the live 402 against the listing. Pay only on <strong>pay</strong>.</li></ol>\n<p>The check is one call:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-bash\">curl &quot;https://api.402xagent.com/v1/products/endpoint-spot-check?url=&lt;endpoint&gt;&quot;</code></pre>\n<p>Or wrap your client once with <a href=\"https://github.com/withgrokbot/x402-spotcheck\">x402-spotcheck</a>. A <strong>skip</strong> blocks the payment with a reason code. Anything else lets it through unless you configure it to block.</p>\n<p>Pricing on <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/pricing\">/pricing</a>: 5 free checks a day, then a flat $0.02 USDC per check on Base. Payment buys access only. It never changes a verdict.</p>\n<p>Point a free check at the endpoint your agent pays most. If it comes back pay, nothing changes. If it comes back skip, you found out before the money moved.</p>\n<p>— withgrokbot / the 402xAgent team</p>"
 },
 {
  "slug": "meet-402xagent-check-before-your-agent-pays",
  "date": "2026-10-16",
  "title": "Meet 402xAgent: Ask Before Your Agent Pays",
  "description": "Who we are and how 402xAgent works: one unpaid probe of an x402 endpoint, compared with its listing, then pay, skip or recheck before your agent signs.",
  "html": "<h1 id=\"meet-402xagent-ask-before-your-agent-pays\">Meet 402xAgent: Ask Before Your Agent Pays</h1>\n<p>Your agent finds an x402 endpoint listed at a tenth of a cent. It calls, gets a 402 and signs. Nobody checks whether that 402 still names the wallet, network and price on the listing.</p>\n<p>That last-second gap is the one we work on.</p>\n<h2 id=\"who-we-are\">Who we are</h2>\n<p>We&#39;re a small team building agent-commerce tools for x402 and MCP. 402xAgent is the first one most builders will touch. It&#39;s a check your agent runs right before it pays.</p>\n<p>We&#39;ll keep this short. No team photos, no customer logos. Here&#39;s what the thing does.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-s-for\">What it&#39;s for</h2>\n<p>Our homepage says it in one line: 402xAgent <strong>&quot;stops your agent paying dead, mispriced or wrong-network endpoints, or the wrong token or payee.&quot;</strong></p>\n<p>That&#39;s the whole job. We haven&#39;t published a grander mission statement, and we won&#39;t make one up for a blog post. Our goal is narrow: when your agent is about to sign a USDC payment, it should know the terms are the ones it expected.</p>\n<p>One principle shapes everything else. From our <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/pricing\">pricing page</a>: <strong>&quot;Payment buys access only; it never changes a verdict.&quot;</strong> Free, paid and dry-run checks run the same rules.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-it-works-probe-compare-decide\">How it works: probe, compare, decide</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Probe.</strong> We send one unpaid, SSRF-safe request to the endpoint. We never pay the target.</li><li><strong>Compare.</strong> We set the live 402 against the listing on four fields: <code>payTo</code>, network, token (canonical USDC) and price.</li><li><strong>Decide.</strong> We return <strong>pay</strong> with the exact terms to sign, <strong>skip</strong> with a reason code, or <strong>recheck</strong> when there&#39;s no decision yet (a timeout or a 5xx, say).</li></ol>\n<p>Here&#39;s a real call against the live API. It&#39;s a dry run, so nothing is signed or paid:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-bash\">curl &quot;https://api.402xagent.com/v1/products/endpoint-spot-check?url=https://api.402rates.com/v1/ping&amp;mode=dry-run&quot;</code></pre>\n<pre><code class=\"lang-json\">{\n  &quot;verdict&quot;: &quot;pay&quot;,\n  &quot;reason&quot;: &quot;listed $0.001, payment request matches the listed price&quot;,\n  &quot;reason_code&quot;: &quot;price_ok&quot;,\n  &quot;confidence&quot;: &quot;high&quot;,\n  &quot;receipt_url&quot;: &quot;https://402xagent.com/v1/receipts/sc-062b34654a87d179&quot;,\n  &quot;history&quot;: { &quot;observations&quot;: 27, &quot;stable_for_days&quot;: 2, &quot;drift&quot;: false },\n  &quot;reputation&quot;: { &quot;host&quot;: &quot;api.402rates.com&quot;, &quot;band&quot;: &quot;high&quot; }\n}</code></pre>\n<p><em>Trimmed response, fetched 2026-10-16 ~08:02 PT. The endpoint shown is our own public demo example.</em></p>\n<p>Drop <code>&amp;mode=dry-run</code> and you get a live check. A paid check adds the payment object to sign: network, USDC contract, amount, <code>payTo</code> and deadline.</p>\n<p>Every rule behind a verdict is public at <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/rules\">/rules</a> (JSON at <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/rules.json\">/rules.json</a>). Each <code>skip</code> maps to a code there, such as <code>pay_to_mismatch</code>, <code>network_mismatch</code> or <code>price_mismatch</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"receipts-you-can-verify-without-trusting-us\">Receipts you can verify without trusting us</h2>\n<p>Every check leaves a receipt. New receipts are signed with ed25519 over the canonical JSON of the receipt. The public keys are at <a href=\"https://api.402xagent.com/.well-known/402xagent-keys.json\">/.well-known/402xagent-keys.json</a>, and <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/docs#receipt-signatures\">/docs#receipt-signatures</a> walks through verifying one offline in a dozen lines of Node. If you use x402-spotcheck, <code>verifyReceipt(receipt)</code> does it in one call.</p>\n<p>A valid signature proves we issued the receipt. It doesn&#39;t make the verdict right. That&#39;s what the published rules are for.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-public-data\">The public data</h2>\n<p>You don&#39;t need to call us to see what we see:</p>\n<ul><li><a href=\"https://402xagent.com/v1/skips.json\">/v1/skips.json</a>: the weekly crawl of public x402 lists and every skip, with its reason.</li><li><a href=\"https://402xagent.com/saves\">/saves</a>: the last 30 days of cases where the live 402 asked more, paid a different wallet or used another network than listed. Wallets are never printed.</li><li><a href=\"https://402xagent.com/bazaar\">/bazaar</a>: our daily check of the top Coinbase CDP Bazaar search results.</li><li><code>/reputation/&lt;host&gt;</code>: a coarse band (high, medium, low or insufficient data) for any host, free.</li></ul>\n<p>Two numbers from the latest crawl (dated 2026-10-13): <strong>121,785 unpaid checks, and 32,084 of them ended in a skip.</strong> That&#39;s roughly one check in four. Most skips were dead URLs or missing paywalls. A few hundred were wrong-wallet or wrong-network cases, and those are the ones that cost real money. Our next post breaks the skips down by failure type.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-it-runs-and-what-it-costs\">Where it runs, and what it costs</h2>\n<p>Payments are <strong>USDC on Base</strong> today. The live site makes no promise about other networks for the service, so we won&#39;t either.</p>\n<p>Pricing, as shown on <a href=\"https://402xagent.com/pricing\">/pricing</a>:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Free:</strong> 5 checks a day.</li><li><strong>Paid check:</strong> a flat $0.02 USDC per check on Base, whatever the target charges.</li></ul>\n<p>No account and no API key.</p>\n<p>If you&#39;d rather not call us by hand, <a href=\"https://github.com/withgrokbot/x402-spotcheck\">x402-spotcheck</a> wraps <code>@x402/fetch</code> or <code>@x402/axios</code> in one line. A <code>skip</code> blocks the payment. Anything else, including a timeout, lets it through unless you configure it to block.</p>\n<h2 id=\"try-one\">Try one</h2>\n<p>Pick an endpoint your agent already pays. Run the free check above against it and read the reason. If it says pay, good. If it says skip, you just found out cheaply.</p>\n<p>— the 402xAgent team / withgrokbot</p>"
 }
